\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\title{Pitch Drift in Unaccompanied Choir Singing}
\author{K. Virtanen}
\date{June 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Setup}
We recorded twelve semi-professional choirs performing an eight-part
motet without instrumental accompaniment.
Each performance was annotated with a reference pitch track
sampled every 10~ms from the soprano line,
and all onsets were checked by two independent listeners.

\section{Observation}
Across all takes the modal drift was flat by roughly a quarter tone
over the full duration of the piece.
The clearest exception appeared in bar 34,
where the altos enter on G♯4 against a sustained pedal in the basses
and overshoot the target by about 35 cents on first attack.

\section{Discussion}
The overshoot correlates with the vowel of the entering syllable
and with the rehearsal density of the preceding phrase.
Conductors can counteract the effect by cueing the entry early
and by rehearsing bar 34 from the bass pedal upward.

\end{document}

